{#
  The delete confirmation — the Twig counterpart of
  module/SionModel/view/sion-model/sion-model/delete.phtml, which still renders this page
  wherever laminas is the front controller.

  **One template for all seven routes**, where the edit surface needed ten. That is not a
  simplification taken here; it is how the laminas page is built. `deleteAction()` renders
  one view script for every entity, and the only thing that varies is the entity key in the
  heading. There is no breadcrumb trail, no per-entity partial and no record name — the page
  does not even tell the visitor *which* record they are about to destroy, which is a
  usability problem filed in docs/BACKLOG.md rather than fixed in a port.

  ## Three details worth stating, because each is load-bearing

  - **`entityObject` is passed and ignored.** The laminas action fetches the row
    (`getEntityObject($id)`) and puts it in the view model, and the view script never reads
    it. This template reproduces the rendering, so the controller does not fetch a row it
    would only discard.
  - **The `&nbsp;` between the two buttons is content, not layout.** delete.phtml echoes the
    submit, then `&nbsp;`, then the button, with nothing between them. tools/port-baseline.php
    collapses ordinary whitespace but deliberately preserves U+00A0, so a newline here would
    be forgiven and a stray space around the entity would not — hence the whitespace control.
  - **The heading is translated, and the string is assembled.** `'Delete ' ~ entity` is the
    literal laminas builds and passes through the translator, so the phrase rows already
    exist — `Delete association` under the `Schoenstatt` domain, `Delete publication` under
    `Books`, and five more. The controller hands the assembled string over as `page_title` so
    that the `<h1>` here and the `<title>` in the layout resolve the *same* phrase in the
    route's own text domain. Splitting them would file a second row under `default`.

  ## Cancel does not delete any more

  Until 2026-08-14 the Cancel button below rendered as `type="submit"`: SionModel's
  `DeleteEntityForm` added it with no element type, and `FormButton` defaults a typeless
  element to a submit. `deleteAction()` validated the CSRF token without looking at which
  button was pressed, so **clicking Cancel deleted the record** — measured against a fixture,
  302 to the entity index with the row gone. The form now declares `Button`, whose own
  attributes carry `type="button"`, and both this route and the laminas action refuse a POST
  naming `cancel`.

  The consequence for this page is that Cancel no longer navigates anywhere: it is inert
  outside a modal, where `data-dismiss` gives it its job back. An inert button is worse than
  a useful one and much better than a destructive one; giving it somewhere to go needs a
  cancel URL the shared form does not currently have, and is filed.
#}
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}

{% block content %}
<h1>{{ translate(page_title) }}</h1>
{{ form_open(form, form_action) }}
{{- form_row(form.get('security')) -}}
{{- form_submit(form.get('submit')) -}}
&nbsp;{{- form_button(form.get('cancel')) -}}
{{ form_close() }}
{% endblock %}
